# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# Required status checks for main. A declaration beats discovery exactly.
#
# Discovery requires checks BY JOB NAME, which is a guess at the PR gate and
# drifts silently: a renamed job, an added workflow, or any future check
# reporting under the github-actions app changes what is required without
# anyone deciding to. Pinning it costs nothing and makes the intent reviewable.
#
# WHAT THIS PINS IS A LIST THAT NOBODY REVIEWS. Until now the six names below
# lived only in the repository settings page, typed in by hand, with no diff,
# no author and no reviewer. They happen to be correct today -- the audit in
# antimatter-studios/diskjockey#197 checked, and the check-runs on main's HEAD
# and on the newest pull_request check-suite are exactly these six, all
# success. That is the state this file freezes. The next job added to ci.yml
# is the one that matters: it starts reporting on every pull request, nothing
# requires it, a red one merges, and the only trace is a settings page nobody
# opens. The sibling rust-img-qcow2 was already in that state, producing a
# `test / i686-unknown-linux-gnu` check that was never required by anyone.
#
# ONLY ci.yml CAN GATE A MERGE, and this repository makes that easy to get
# wrong: release.yml runs jobs named `test` and `qemu-validation` too, but it
# triggers on a `v*.*.*` tag, which happens AFTER the merge. A required check
# that only a tag can produce never reports on a pull request and blocks the
# repository permanently, so none of release.yml's jobs are declared here.
# `qemu-validation` below is ci.yml's job of that name -- the one that runs on
# pull_request -- and the collision is why the distinction is written down.
#
# Third-party reviewers are not in the list and do not need to be excluded by
# hand: github-protect-main.sh filters `select(.app.slug=="github-actions")`
# on every one of its queries, so a review app cannot become required.
#
# Names must match the check-run names GitHub reports EXACTLY, and the two
# spellings here differ from each other on purpose: a matrix leg is
# "test / ubuntu-latest", with spaces around the slash, because Actions joins
# the job's `name:` to the matrix value that way, while `test (release)` is a
# job whose own `name:` contains the parentheses. Neither is a typo, and a
# misspelling of either is caught rather than enforced -- the guard keeps a
# declared name only once it has concluded success on main, precisely so a
# name that can never report cannot lock the branch.
#
# NO AGGREGATE GATE YET. The better end state is one always-run `ci-ok` job
# that `needs:` every gating job and fails on failure, cancelled or skipped,
# so the required list is one name and adding a job cannot escape the gate.
# That is a change to ci.yml's shape and belongs in its own pull request; the
# list is enumerated here in the meantime.
[checks]
	required = fmt
	required = test / ubuntu-latest
	required = test / macos-latest
	required = test / windows-latest
	required = test (release)
	required = qemu-validation
